<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="ch-cs-planner" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Computer science four-year planner</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The same planner for the computer science major. Prerequisites and the
      2026<ndash/>27 quarter offerings come from the department's CS advising
      document rather than the Bulletin, and the department's sequencing advice is
      built in: <c>MATH 122</c> as soon after <c>MATH 14</c> as possible,
      <c>CSCI 161</c> and <c>163</c> not long after <c>MATH 51</c>, <c>CSCI 62</c>
      before any upper-division CSCI course, and at least one programming-heavy
      upper-division course in sophomore year. Where the advice is not followed the
      plan says so under <q>Warnings and advice</q>.
      For comfortable use, <url href="external/cs-planner.html" visual="">open the planner full size in a new tab</url>.
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="note-cs-advising">
      <title>Important</title>
      <p>
        A plan made here is a draft. Discuss it with your advisor and go over it
        together in an advising session before you finalize it.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-cs-planner">
    <title>The planner</title>
    <interactive xml:id="cs-planner" iframe="cs-planner.html" width="100%" aspect="2:3">
      <instructions>
        <p>
          It works like the mathematics planner: students build their own plan,
          AP (Advanced Placement), IB (International Baccalaureate), or transfer
          credit is ticked separately, and the
          <q>Sample four-year plan</q> suggests the rest once a course has been added, in the order of the department's sample curriculum (Fall 2025). With the emphasis
          left as <q>Undecided</q>, the recommendations fill the five emphasis
          slots with programming-heavy courses.
          Core courses, and electives the menus do not list such as other CSEN
          courses, can be added with <q>+ Type a course…</q>. An upper-division CSCI
          course below 190 or CSEN course below 188 added this way counts toward the
          emphasis courses; the planner does not check prerequisites or offering
          quarters for typed-in courses.
          <q>Download plan (PDF)</q> prints only the plan.
        </p>
      </instructions>
      <static><p>Interactive four-year planner (available in the HTML version).</p></static>
    </interactive>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-cs-planner-offerings">
    <title>Course offerings</title>
    <p>Where the quarters for each course come from:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>
        Upper-division CSCI and MATH courses, including the alternate-year rotation:
        the department's <url href="https://www.scu.edu/cas/mathcs/academic-program/upper-division-course-schedule/" visual="scu.edu/cas/mathcs/academic-program/upper-division-course-schedule">upper-division course schedule</url> (revised May 2026).
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        Lower-division CSCI courses and MATH 51, 52, 53 and 122: the tentative
        2026<ndash/>27 list in the department's <url href="https://www.scu.edu/media/college-of-arts-and-sciences/math-and-cs/documents/CS-Advising-Help-accessible.pdf" visual="scu.edu/media/college-of-arts-and-sciences/math-and-cs/documents/CS-Advising-Help-accessible.pdf">CS advising document</url>. The department
        confirmed CSCI 10 in Fall and Winter and CSCI 60, 61 and 62 every quarter
        (October 2026).
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        Calculus (MATH 11<ndash/>14): every quarter, confirmed by the department.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        The science course: every quarter (PHYS 31 Winter and Spring, CHEM 11 and
        ENVS 21 Fall and Winter, ENVS 23 Fall and Spring), from the University's class
        listings for 2024<ndash/>25 through Fall 2026, the same listings shown on the
        department class schedules (for example the Physics <url href="https://www.scu.edu/cas/physics-and-engineering-physics/academic-program/class-schedule/" visual="scu.edu/cas/physics-and-engineering-physics/academic-program/class-schedule">class schedule</url>).
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        Precalculus and the CSEN and ECEN courses, from the same class listings for
        2023<ndash/>24 through Fall 2026: CSEN 20, 146, 161, 166, 177 and ECEN 21
        every quarter, CSEN 169 in Winter, and CSEN 178 in Fall and Spring.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        Never suggested: CSEN 152, which has not run since at least 2024, and
        CSCI 127 and 165, which are offered by request.
      </p></li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>

</section>
